## Executive Summary

Using devices such as *Jawbone Up*, *Nike FuelBand*, and *Fitbit*, it is now possible to collect a large amount of data about personal activity relatively inexpensively. In this project, our goal is to use data from accelerometers on the belt, forearm, arm, and dumbell of 6 participants to predict the manner in which they performed the exercise (`classe` variable). We build and evaluate a **Random Forest** prediction model using 5-fold cross-validation.

## Data Loading and Preprocessing

```r
library(caret)
library(randomForest)

set.seed(12345)

train_url <- "[https://d396qusza40orc.cloudfront.net/predmachlearn/pml-training.csv](https://d396qusza40orc.cloudfront.net/predmachlearn/pml-training.csv)"
test_url  <- "[https://d396qusza40orc.cloudfront.net/predmachlearn/pml-testing.csv](https://d396qusza40orc.cloudfront.net/predmachlearn/pml-testing.csv)"

# Load datasets treating blanks and error strings as NA
training_raw <- read.csv(train_url, na.strings = c("NA", "#DIV/0!", ""))
testing_raw  <- read.csv(test_url,  na.strings = c("NA", "#DIV/0!", ""))

training_raw$classe <- as.factor(training_raw$classe)
```

### Data Cleaning

We remove columns containing mostly missing values, non-predictive metadata (IDs and timestamps), and near-zero variance predictors.

```r
# Remove columns with >60% missing values
na_threshold <- 0.6 * nrow(training_raw)
cols_to_keep <- colSums(is.na(training_raw)) < na_threshold
training_clean <- training_raw[, cols_to_keep]
testing_clean  <- testing_raw[, cols_to_keep]

# Remove identification/timestamp columns (first 7)
training_clean <- training_clean[, -(1:7)]
testing_clean  <- testing_clean[, -(1:7)]

# Remove near-zero variance variables
nzv <- nearZeroVar(training_clean)
if(length(nzv) > 0) {
  training_clean <- training_clean[, -nzv]
  testing_clean  <- testing_clean[, -nzv]
}
```

## Cross-Validation Setup & Model Training

We split the cleaned training data into a **70% training set** and a **30% validation set**. Cross-validation is set up with 5 folds within the training process.

```r
inTrain <- createDataPartition(y = training_clean$classe, p = 0.70, list = FALSE)
train_set <- training_clean[inTrain, ]
valid_set <- training_clean[-inTrain, ]

# Set 5-fold cross-validation
train_control <- trainControl(method = "cv", number = 5, verboseIter = FALSE)

# Train Random Forest
rf_model <- train(classe ~ ., data = train_set, method = "rf", trControl = train_control)
```

## Out-of-Sample Error Evaluation

We evaluate the trained model on our validation dataset to estimate performance.

```r
predictions_valid <- predict(rf_model, newdata = valid_set)
conf_matrix <- confusionMatrix(predictions_valid, valid_set$classe)
conf_matrix
```

```r
accuracy <- conf_matrix$overall['Accuracy']
out_of_sample_error <- 1 - accuracy
```

The model achieved an accuracy of **`r round(accuracy * 100, 2)`%** on the validation set. Consequently, the estimated **out-of-sample error** is **`r round(out_of_sample_error * 100, 2)`%**, demonstrating high predictive quality.

## Final Quiz Predictions

Finally, we apply our trained Random Forest model to predict the 20 test cases.

```r
